  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 145 PM CDT WED SEP 16 2026\n\nA large upper ridge currently centered over Tennessee that is\nsuppressing convection across Central Alabama is expected to\nretrograde over the next couple of days. By Friday, the ridge\nshould extend from Arkansas to Central Texas. This shift will\noccur in conjunction with a bit of energy breaking off over the\nWestern Atlantic from the main northern upper flow. This\nshortwave is expected to form a closed low over the next couple \nof days over Florida, slowly nudge westward, and then help to push\nand degrade the ridge. What does this mean for Central Alabama? \nWe will continue with hot and dry weather over the next couple of \ndays. Over the weekend as the upper low moves closer to us, low \nrain chances will return by Saturday, first in the eastern \ncounties, as this area will be closest to the low with the best \nfocus. However, initially, this weekend, overall moisture will be \nlimited, so QPF amounts are low. For Monday into Tuesday, guidance\nbrings the upper low further westward over the Northern Gulf \nCoast. This will begin to allow for better moisture to filter into\nthe state. Low to medium chances for convection will occur by \nthis point with the probability of slightly higher rain amounts \nfor a lucky few, but not enough to alleviate current dry/drought \nconditions. However, the better rain chances will allow for high \ntemperatures a couple of degrees lower.\n\n08",
